This is a common issue that happens when opening HTML files locally due to browser security restrictions (CORS and file protocol limitations). The good news is it will work perfectly when deployed to Netlify or any web server.

The Problem

When you open the file directly (file:///C:/...), browsers block some JavaScript features like localStorage access across different file contexts, and certain event handlers may behave differently. The sidebar tabs rely on JavaScript to switch between sections, which works fine over HTTP/HTTPS but can be inconsistent locally.

Quick Fix for Local Testing

Add this script right after your opening <body> tag to ensure local file compatibility:

```html
<script>
    // Fix for local file protocol
    if (window.location.protocol === 'file:') {
        document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
            // Re-attach sidebar click handlers with delegation
            document.querySelector('.sidebar')?.addEventListener('click', function(e) {
                const link = e.target.closest('.nav-link[data-target]');
                if (link && link.getAttribute('data-target')) {
                    e.preventDefault();
                    const target = link.getAttribute('data-target');
                    if (window.navigateTo) window.navigateTo(target);
                }
            });
            
            // Ensure Bootstrap JS is loaded
            if (typeof bootstrap === 'undefined') {
                console.warn('Bootstrap not loaded - check CDN connection');
            }
        });
    }
</script>
```

Why It Works on Netlify

Once deployed to Netlify:

· Files serve over HTTPS (secure protocol)
· All JavaScript features work normally
· localStorage persists correctly
· CDN resources load properly
· No CORS or file protocol restrictions

Deployment Checklist for Netlify

1. Create a netlify.toml file in your project root:

```toml
[[headers]]
  for = "/*"
  [headers.values]
    X-Frame-Options = "DENY"
    X-XSS-Protection = "1; mode=block"
```

1. Ensure all assets use HTTPS (they already do - good!)
2. Test before deploying - use VS Code's Live Server extension or run:

```bash
npx serve .
```

The Real Issue

Looking at your code, the sidebar uses data-target attributes and your navigateTo function is attached to window. The local file protocol sometimes delays script execution or causes event binding order issues. Your code is structurally sound - it's purely a local file limitation.

Bottom line: Your code is fine. Deploy to Netlify (drag and drop the folder) and it will work perfectly. For local development, consider using VS Code's Live Server or Python's http.server instead of double-clicking the file.
